Common triggers listed by the experts are:
- certain foods and alcohol;
- strong smells;
- temperature changes;
- friction or rubbing on the skin;
- emotional stress;
- some medications, such as opioids, anti-inflammatory painkillers (NSAIDs) and the iodine-based contrast used in some scans.

Your triggers may be different, so keeping a symptom diary can help you spot them. Treatments include antihistamines (which block histamine) and mast cell stabilizers (which calm mast cells). If MCAS is likely, you should be referred to an allergy specialist or a mast cell center. Before any scan with contrast or any procedure, tell the team about your MCAS.

Conservative options:
- physical activity;
- acetaminophen and NSAIDs;
- heat and/or cold;
- better ergonomics and posture;
- relaxation techniques;
- massage and hydrotherapy;
- joint stabilization with bracing and/or taping.

Avoid medications that impair platelet function in patients with hEDS who bruise easily. Check the bleeding history before prescribing.

Generalized hypermobility (four or more joints) is found in 12–28% of children, adolescents and young adults and is often uncomplicated. In dancers, gymnasts, wrestlers, cheerleaders and similar athletes, hypermobility may be acquired through training and does not by itself indicate a connective tissue disorder.

Look for features beyond flexibility before considering hEDS or HSD:
- recurrent atraumatic instability;
- chronic pain;
- systemic connective tissue signs;
- family history.

Compared with individuals without HSD/hEDS, prevalence is markedly higher:
- abdominal pain: 69% versus 27%;
- postprandial fullness: 34% versus 16%;
- constipation: 73% versus 16%;
- diarrhea: 47% versus 9%.

Disorders of gut-brain interaction are common, especially functional dyspepsia.

Asking about GI symptoms in hypermobile patients, and about hypermobility in patients with these GI complaints, is therefore worthwhile.

All included studies described a substantial emotional impact:
- constant anxiety and fear of the future;
- catastrophizing;
- anger and grief over pain and limitations, which one patient compared to a bereavement.

Frustration with providers can lead patients to avoid healthcare unless they are desperate. Acknowledge these responses, screen for anxiety and depression, and maintain engagement, so that avoidance does not delay needed care.

JHS/EDS-HT was found in:
- 33% of unselected patients referred to a secondary-care gastroenterology clinic;
- 39% of patients with functional GI disorders, versus 27.5% of those with organic disease;
- 51% of patients with postprandial distress syndrome, a subtype of functional dyspepsia.

Patients with functional dyspepsia, particularly postprandial distress, and other functional GI disorders are a high-yield group for screening with the Beighton score and a few history questions.
